  <!-- 1 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="what">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The idea</p>
      <h2>What a set is</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def">
        <p class="note-label">Set</p>
        <p>A <b>set</b> is an abstract data type (ADT, explained below) that stores a collection of values with <b>no duplicates</b> and <b>no fixed order</b>. The main question a set answers is: <i>"Is this value in the collection or not?"</i></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={setVsList()} /><figcaption>The same four names go into a list and into a set. The list keeps the second Ana. The set keeps each name once.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="sub" id="adt">
        <h3>What is an abstract data type (ADT)?</h3>
        <p>Every data structure can be described by answering two questions:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>What can I do with it?</b> Its operations, and what each one gives back. For a set: add a value, remove a value, ask "Is this value in it?", and count the values.</li>
          <li><b>How is it stored inside the computer?</b> For a set, it could be an array, a tree, or a hash table. Any of these can work.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>An <b>abstract data type (ADT)</b> answers only the first question. "Abstract" means "the idea only, without the details". So the ADT says <i>what</i> you can do, not <i>how</i> it is done. The second question is answered by the code that builds it, called the <b>implementation</b>.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={adtStore()} /><figcaption>A sari-sari store works like an ADT. At the window, you can only ask (contains?) or ask for changes (add, remove). How the stock is arranged inside is hidden, and two stores can do it differently. Both still answer "Do you have Coke?" correctly.</figcaption></figure>
        <p>This table matches the store to a set:</p>
        <div class="scroll-x"><table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>In the store</th><th>In a set</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>You ask: "Do you have Coke?"</td><td><code>contains("Coke")</code>: the answer is yes or no (true or false)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>New stock arrives</td><td><code>add("Coke")</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>The last one is sold</td><td><code>remove("Coke")</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>How the shelves or bins are arranged (you can't see it)</td><td>how the set is built inside: the implementation</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p><b>Why split it this way?</b> You can use a set without knowing how it is built, just as you can buy at any sari-sari store without seeing its shelves. And the way it is built can be changed later (to make it faster) without changing the code that uses it.</p>
        <p>You already met this idea in the <a href={base + '/itc120/stacks/'}>Stacks</a> lesson: what made a stack a stack was the rule "push, pop, peek", not whether it was built with an array or a linked list. Now the idea has a name. A set is described the same way: "add, remove, contains".</p>
        <p class="small muted">Good to know: this course calls stacks and queues <b>data structures</b> and sets and maps <b>abstract data types</b>. Many books call stacks and queues ADTs too, because they can also be described only by what you can do with them. Both views are correct.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub">
        <h3>The two properties</h3>
        <p>Every set has these two properties:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Uniqueness.</b> Each value appears at most once (like the second Ana in the <Peek label="list vs. set picture" title="From above" html={`<div class=\"illus\">${setVsList()}</div>`} />). Adding a value that is already there does nothing.</li>
          <li><b>Unordered.</b> Items have no position (no place in line). In a list or an array, every item has an <b>index</b>: <code>[0]</code> is the first item, <code>[1]</code> is the second, and so on. A set has no index, so it has no "first" item. You can only ask "Is this value in the set?" That is why &#123;1, 2, 3&#125; and &#123;3, 1, 2&#125; are the <b>same set</b>.</li>
        </ol>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={setOrder()} /><figcaption>Rows 1 and 2: changing the order makes a different list, but the same set. Row 3: a list can give you item [0] (its first item). A set cannot, because its values have no places, so you ask whether a value is in it instead.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="sticky">
        <p class="note-label">Think of the attendance list</p>
        <p>When students tap their ID at the door, some tap twice. But the attendance should list each student <b>once</b>, and it does not matter who came first. That is a set. Compare with the queue at the canteen: there, order matters and the same person can line up again. That is a list or a queue, not a set.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Where you see sets in IT</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Counting <b>unique visitors</b> to a website (the same person visiting 5 times counts once).</li>
          <li>Tags on a post, or the list of permissions a user has.</li>
          <li>Comparing two class lists: who is enrolled in both subjects? (intersection)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={attendanceSet()} /><figcaption>Top: Ana tapped her ID twice, but the attendance lists her once. Bottom: the same idea in IT. Repeat visits count once, a tag appears once per post, and the overlap of two class lists shows who is in both.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="sub" id="subsets">
        <h3>Review: subsets <span class="xtra">(extra)</span></h3>
        <p class="xtra-note"><b>Extra topic.</b> Subsets go a little beyond this course's required set topics (properties, union, intersection, difference, symmetric difference). Study it if you have time; your teacher will tell you if a quiz or exam uses it.</p>
        <p>You may remember subsets from math class. Here is a quick review, because Java can check them too (Part 3).</p>
        <div class="def">
          <p class="note-label">Subset</p>
          <p>A is a <b>subset</b> of B, written <b>A ⊆ B</b>, when every value in A is also in B.</p>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig"><Fragment set:html={subsetPic()} /><figcaption>A = &#123;Ana, Ben&#125; sits inside B = &#123;Ana, Ben, Carla, Dan&#125;, so A ⊆ B. But C = &#123;Ana, Eve&#125; is <b>not</b> a subset of B, because Eve is not in B.</figcaption></figure>
        <p>Here are the words and symbols that go with subsets, using the sets in the picture:</p>
        <div class="scroll-x"><table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><b>Subset</b></td><td class="mono">A ⊆ B</td><td>every value of A is in B</td><td>&#123;Ana, Ben&#125; ⊆ B</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Proper subset</b></td><td class="mono">A ⊂ B</td><td>A ⊆ B, and B has at least one value that A does not</td><td>&#123;Ana, Ben&#125; ⊂ B (B also has Carla and Dan)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Not a subset</b></td><td class="mono">C ⊄ B</td><td>at least one value of C is not in B</td><td>&#123;Ana, Eve&#125; ⊄ B (Eve)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><b>Equal sets</b></td><td class="mono">A = B</td><td>A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A: exactly the same values</td><td>&#123;Ana, Ben&#125; = &#123;Ben, Ana&#125;</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>Two facts are easy to forget:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The empty set &#123; &#125; (the set with nothing in it) is a subset of <b>every</b> set. It has no value that could be missing from B.</li>
          <li>Every set is a subset of <b>itself</b> (B ⊆ B), but not a <i>proper</i> subset of itself.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="trap">
          <p class="note-label">Exam trap: one value vs. a set</p>
          <p><b>Ana ∈ B</b> means "the value Ana is in B" (∈ = "is an element of"). <b>&#123;Ana&#125; ⊆ B</b> means "the set holding only Ana is a subset of B". Use ∈ for one value and ⊆ for a set. Writing "Ana ⊆ B" is wrong.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="plain">
          <p class="note-label">Good to know: ⊂ in other books</p>
          <p>Some books write ⊂ for <i>any</i> subset (what we write ⊆). If a question uses ⊂, check how that book or teacher defines it. Rosen (reference 3) uses ⊆ and ⊂ the same way as this lesson.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="ops">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · Combining sets</p>
      <h2>Set operations</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Set operations take two sets and produce a new set. There are four types you need to know. In the examples, A = &#123;1, 2, 3, 4, 5&#125; and B = &#123;4, 5, 6, 7&#125;.</p>
      <div class="scroll-x"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Union</b></td><td class="mono">A ∪ B</td><td>in A <b>or</b> B (or both)</td><td class="mono">&#123;1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7&#125;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Intersection</b></td><td class="mono">A ∩ B</td><td>in A <b>and</b> B</td><td class="mono">&#123;4, 5&#125;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Difference</b></td><td class="mono">A − B</td><td>in A but <b>not</b> in B</td><td class="mono">&#123;1, 2, 3&#125;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Difference</b></td><td class="mono">B − A</td><td>in B but <b>not</b> in A</td><td class="mono">&#123;6, 7&#125;</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Symmetric difference</b></td><td class="mono">A △ B</td><td>in A or B, but <b>not both</b></td><td class="mono">&#123;1, 2, 3, 6, 7&#125;</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>The lab below draws each operation on a <b>Venn diagram</b>: one circle per set. Values in both sets go in the overlap in the middle; values in only one set go in that circle's outer part. The shaded part is the answer.</p>
      <SetLab id="setlab" a={[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]} b={[4, 5, 6, 7]} />
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Exam traps</p>
        <ol class="trap-list">
          <li><p><b>A − B is not the same as B − A.</b> Order matters for difference: A − B is the part of A outside B, and B − A is the part of B outside A.</p><Fragment set:html={setTrapPic(1)} /></li>
          <li><p>In a union, shared values appear <b>once</b>: &#123;1, 2&#125; ∪ &#123;2, 3&#125; = &#123;1, 2, 3&#125;, not &#123;1, 2, 2, 3&#125;.</p><Fragment set:html={setTrapPic(2)} /></li>
          <li><p>When the result has nothing in it, write the <b>empty set</b>: &#123; &#125; (or ∅). Here A and B have the same values, so their circles sit on top of each other and A − B leaves nothing. Try making A and B equal in the lab.</p><Fragment set:html={setTrapPic(3)} /></li>
          <li><p>A △ B = (A ∪ B) − (A ∩ B) = (A − B) ∪ (B − A). All three give the same answer: the two outer parts, without the middle.</p><Fragment set:html={setTrapPic(4)} /></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="plain">
        <p class="note-label">Good to know: other symbols in other books</p>
        <p>Some books write the difference A − B as <b>A \ B</b>, and the symmetric difference A △ B as <b>A ⊕ B</b> (Rosen, reference 3) or <b>A Δ B</b>. They mean the same operations.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="java">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · In code</p>
      <h2>Sets in Java</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Remember the <Peek label="sari-sari store" title="From Part 1" html={storePeek} />: what you can ask is one thing, and how the stock is arranged inside is another. Java splits a set into the same two parts:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>What a set can do:</b> <code>Set</code> is an <Peek label="interface" title="Interface" html={interfacePeek} />. It lists the methods every set must have (<code>add</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>remove</code>, <code>size</code>, and more), but has no code inside. This is the ADT.</li>
        <li><b>How it is built:</b> <code>HashSet</code>, <code>LinkedHashSet</code>, and <code>TreeSet</code> are <Peek label="classes" title="Class (from the Linked lists lesson)" html={classPeek} />. Each one writes the real code for those methods in its own way. We say each class <b>implements</b> <code>Set</code>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Here are the three main classes:</p>
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Built with</th><th>Order when you loop through it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>HashSet</code></td><td>a hash table, through a <code>HashMap</code> inside (next lesson)</td><td>no guaranteed order</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>LinkedHashSet</code></td><td>a hash table + a linked list</td><td>the order you added them</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>TreeSet</code></td><td>a red-black tree, through a <code>TreeMap</code> inside</td><td>sorted</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>Wait: <Peek label="Part 1" title="From Part 1" html={setDefPeek} /> said a set has no order. The set <b>ADT</b> promises no order, so you can never ask for "item [2]". <code>LinkedHashSet</code> and <code>TreeSet</code> add an order as an extra, only for looping through the values. They still have no index, and they still keep each value once.</p>
      <p>You will see both parts in one line of code: <code>Set&lt;String&gt; present = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();</code> reads as "present is a <b>Set</b> of Strings, built as a <b>HashSet</b>." The left side (<code>Set</code>) says what you may do with it. The right side (<code>new HashSet&lt;&gt;()</code>) picks how it is built. To get sorted order instead, you would change only the right side, to <code>new TreeSet&lt;&gt;()</code>.</p>
      <p><code>HashSet</code> is the usual choice. Its basic methods are <code>add</code>, <code>contains</code>, <code>remove</code>, and <code>size</code>:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(basics)} /></pre>
      <TryIt cls="Attendance" />

      <div class="sub">
        <h3>Set operations in Java</h3>
        <p>Java does not have methods named "union" or "intersection". Instead, three methods do the work. Each one <b>changes the set it is called on</b>:</p>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What it does to <code>x</code></th><th>Operation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>x.addAll(y)</code></td><td>adds every value of y into x</td><td>union</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>x.retainAll(y)</code></td><td>keeps only the values that are also in y</td><td>intersection</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>x.removeAll(y)</code></td><td>takes out every value that is in y</td><td>difference</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(opsCode)} /></pre>
        <TryIt cls="SetOperations" />
        <p class="small muted">The code prints u, n, and sym instead of ∪, ∩, and △, because some consoles cannot show those symbols. Small whole numbers like these usually come out in increasing order from a <code>HashSet</code>, but that is not guaranteed (the names in the attendance example did not). The values are what matter, not their order.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">The #1 bug: forgetting to copy</p>
        <p>Because <code>addAll</code>, <code>retainAll</code>, and <code>removeAll</code> change the set itself, always start from a <b>copy</b>: <code>new HashSet&lt;&gt;(a)</code>. If you write <code>a.addAll(b)</code> directly, A is now the union, and every later operation that uses A gives the wrong answer.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="paper flow bugs">
        <p class="note-label">Spot the bug</p>
        <p>This code should print A − B and B − A. The B − A part was copied from the A − B part, and two lines were not fully updated. Which lines, and what does the code print?</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(buggy, [9, 10]).replaceAll('class="hl-line"', 'class="bug-line"')} /></pre>
        <details class="reveal">
          <summary>Show the answer</summary>
          <div class="flow">
            <p>Lines 9 and 10 still use <code>aMinusB</code>. Line 9 should be <code>bMinusA.removeAll(a);</code>, and line 10 should print <code>bMinusA</code>.</p>
            <p>As written, it prints <code>A - B: [10, 30]</code>, then removes A's values from <code>aMinusB</code> (which empties it) and prints <code>B - A: []</code>. The correct B − A is <b>&#123;40&#125;</b>.</p>
            <p>This kind of copy-paste mistake is common. When you copy a block of code, check <b>every</b> variable name in it.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="sub" id="subsets-java">
        <h3>Subsets in Java <span class="xtra">(extra)</span></h3>
        <p class="xtra-note"><b>Extra topic,</b> like the <a href="#subsets">subsets review</a> in Part 2.</p>
        <p>Java has no method named "subset". You check <Peek label="A ⊆ B" title="From Part 2" html={subsetPeek} /> with <code>containsAll</code>. This table shows each subset question in Java:</p>
        <div class="scroll-x"><table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>By hand</th><th>In Java</th><th>Read it as</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="mono">A ⊆ B</td><td><code>b.containsAll(a)</code></td><td>"Does b contain all of a?"</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">A ⊂ B</td><td><code>b.containsAll(a) &amp;&amp; !a.equals(b)</code></td><td>"all of a, and not the same set"</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">A = B</td><td><code>a.equals(b)</code></td><td>"exactly the same values?"</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="mono">x ∈ B</td><td><code>b.contains(x)</code></td><td>"is this one value in b?"</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>Watch the direction: A ⊆ B is written <code>b.containsAll(a)</code>, so the <b>bigger</b> set goes first. And unlike <code>addAll</code>, <code>retainAll</code>, and <code>removeAll</code>, <code>containsAll</code> changes nothing. It only answers true or false, so no copy is needed.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(subsetsCode)} /></pre>
        <TryIt cls="Subsets" />
        <p class="small muted"><code>equals</code> ignores order, and even the class: a <code>HashSet</code> and a <code>TreeSet</code> with the same values are equal.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 -->
  <section class="chapter wrap" id="paper">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Your turn</p>
      <h2>Practice</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>For each pair, write A ∪ B, A ∩ B, A − B, B − A, and A △ B. Drawing a quick Venn diagram first makes it easy: put shared values in the middle. After the four problems, a short coding task turns one of these operations into Java.</p>
      {practice.map((p, i) => (
        <div class="paper flow">
          <p class="note-label">Problem {i + 1}</p>
          <p class="mono">A = {fmt(p.A)}<br />B = {fmt(p.B)}</p>
          <details class="reveal">
            <summary>Show the answer</summary>
            <div class="flow">
              <table class="tbl">
                <tbody>
                  <tr><th class="mono">A ∪ B</th><td class="mono">{fmt(p.r.union)}</td></tr>
                  <tr><th class="mono">A ∩ B</th><td class="mono">{fmt(p.r.inter)}</td></tr>
                  <tr><th class="mono">A − B</th><td class="mono">{fmt(p.r.amb)}</td></tr>
                  <tr><th class="mono">B − A</th><td class="mono">{fmt(p.r.bma)}</td></tr>
                  <tr><th class="mono">A △ B</th><td class="mono">{fmt(p.r.sym)}</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
              {p.note && <p>{p.note}</p>}
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      ))}
      <div class="paper flow">
        <p class="note-label">Problem 4: the library tap-in log</p>
        <p>Students tap their ID cards when they enter the library, and they tap again each time they come back from a break. Monday morning's log, in tap order:</p>
        <p class="mono">1021, 1045, 1021, 1102, 1045, 1021, 1230</p>
        <p>Tuesday's different visitors were <span class="mono">&#123;1045, 1230, 1301&#125;</span>.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>How many <b>different</b> students visited on Monday?</li>
          <li>Which students came on <b>both</b> days?</li>
          <li>Which students came on Monday but <b>not</b> on Tuesday?</li>
        </ol>
        <details class="reveal">
          <summary>Show the answer</summary>
          <div class="flow">
            <ol>
              <li>Write the log as a set, keeping each ID once: Monday = <span class="mono">&#123;1021, 1045, 1102, 1230&#125;</span>. That is <b>4</b> different students, even though there were 7 taps.</li>
              <li>Both days = Monday ∩ Tuesday = <span class="mono">&#123;1045, 1230&#125;</span>.</li>
              <li>Monday only = Monday − Tuesday = <span class="mono">&#123;1021, 1102&#125;</span>.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>In Java, the set removes the repeated taps for you. Adding the whole log to a <code>HashSet</code> keeps each ID once:</p>
            <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(libCode)} /></pre>
            <TryIt cls="Library" />
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
      <div class="paper flow" id="task">
        <p class="note-label">Your coding task: A △ B without changing A or B</p>
        <p>In <Peek label="Part 2" title="From Part 2 · Symmetric difference" html={symPeek} />, A △ B was the two outer parts of the Venn diagram: (A − B) ∪ (B − A). Now write it in Java.</p>
        <p>Finish <code>symDiff</code> in about 5 lines where it says YOUR CODE. Use <code>removeAll</code> and <code>addAll</code> from <a href="#java">Part 3</a>, and watch out for the #1 bug: work on <b>copies</b>, so <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> stay the same. The tests use the pairs from Problems 1 to 3 above, so you can compare with your paper answers. Run it: each printed line must match its expected comment.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskCode)} /></pre>
        <TryIt />
        <details class="reveal"><summary>Show one possible answer</summary><div class="flow">
          <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskAns)} /></pre>
          <p>Output, one per line: <code>[2, 3, 4, 8, 9, 10, 12]</code> twice (A △ B and B △ A are the same set), then <code>[2, 4, 6, 8, 10]</code> and <code>[3, 6, 9, 12]</code> (A and B did not change), then <code>[]</code> and <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>.</p>
          <p>Another correct answer follows the Part 3 code: copy A and add all of B (the union), copy A and keep only B's values (the intersection), then remove the intersection from the union.</p>
          <p>Here is the most common mistake: skipping the copies.</p>
          <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(taskMis)} /></pre>
          <p>The first line takes B's values out of A itself, so A is now [2, 4, 8, 10]. The second line removes that changed A from B, which takes out nothing. The last line adds B back, so the method returns [2, 3, 4, <b>6</b>, 8, 9, 10, 12]: 6 is back, even though it is in both sets. And since <code>a</code> itself was changed, the next tests are wrong too. Every line of the first test prints that same wrong set, and Problem 2 prints all four values instead of <code>[]</code>.</p>
        </div></details>
      </div>
    </div>
